Fair value is 10.5, the expected sum of three dice. I compared half-spreads by expected profit per side, counting noise-trader gains against informed-trader losses. A half-spread of about 2.5 does best, at roughly 0.34 per side. Net profit is flat between 2.5 and 3, and lower at 1 and 1.5.

That gives a bid of 8 and an ask of 13, the same as quote 1.

{"bid": 8, "ask": 13}

Die 1 is 6, so the public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. My position is flat (bought at 8, sold at 14.8), with cash of 6.8.

I estimated expected profit per quote at different half-spreads around 13, counting noise traders (valuation about N(13, 3)) and informed traders (who know the exact sum, 8 to 18). The edge peaks at roughly 0.36 per side for a half-spread of about 2 to 3. It drops to about 0.19 at a half-spread of 1, and to about 0.02 at 0.5, because informed traders take too much. I'll quote a half-spread of 2.4, symmetric around 13.

{"bid": 10.6, "ask": 15.4}

Fair value is 6 + 2 + 3.5 = 11.5, since die 3 is the only unknown and averages 3.5. An informed trader's value then lies between 9 and 14.

I compared the expected profit per quote at different spreads around 11.5 (noise traders at 11.5 ± 3, informed trader 20% of the time). The profit peaks at about 2.5 on each side of fair value, which puts the quotes at roughly 9 and 14. At those prices an informed trader can barely profit, because their value never goes beyond 14 or below 9. I'll quote slightly inside that at 9.1 and 13.9 to catch a little more noise flow. My position is 0, so both sides are open.

