I estimated the contract's expected settlement value at about 18.4. The three dice have means of 6.75, 6.8 and 4.875, and the sum has a standard deviation of about 6.5.

Half of the counterparties are informed, so I priced against adverse selection. For a symmetric quote around fair value, the profit-maximising half-spread is about 1.2 standard deviations, roughly 7.5 to 7.8 points. That puts the quote near 10.6 / 26.2, and I tightened it slightly to 11 / 25.8.

{"bid": 11, "ask": 25.8}

My estimate of the settlement value is 18.4, from the dice means 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.875 = 18.425. The standard deviation of the sum is about 6.5, the same as the noise trader's.

With informed and noise traders equally likely, a quote close to fair value loses money on average. Informed counterparties take every mispriced quote, and noise traders don't make up for it. My rough calculation puts the break-even half-spread around 4. Expected profit per side peaks at roughly 7 to 8 away from fair value, so I'm quoting about 7 on each side.

