The three dice have means 3.75, 2.5 and 6.25, so the contract's expected value is 12.5. The variances are 15.19, 8.5 and 9.94, which sum to about 33.6, giving a standard deviation of about 5.8.

Half the counterparties are informed, so a tight spread loses money to them. I modelled the settlement value as roughly normal, with the noise traders' valuations spread like it. Under that model, expected profit per side is highest when the quote sits about 1.1 to 1.3 standard deviations from the mean, which is roughly 6.4 to 7.5 points. I chose about 6.4 points on each side of 12.5.

