The fair value of the max of the three dice is about 9.09. The distribution of the max is 4 (7.5%), 6 (10%), 7 (8.75%), 8 (26.25%), 9 (7.5%) and 12 (40%).

The 40% mass at 12 makes informed buyers costly to sell to, so the ask needs a wide margin. Weighing the noise-trader edge against the informed-trader loss, expected profit per quote peaks at roughly a bid of 7.0 and an ask of 11.4.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 11.4}

Card 1 is 15, the highest card in the deck, so the max of the three cards is certain to be 15. Everyone knows this, so the informed trader has no edge over a noise trader and there is no adverse selection.

Noise traders value the contract at 15 + N(0, 2.7). With a bid at 15 − d, my expected profit per arrival is about d · Φ(−d/2.7). This is maximised near d ≈ 2.0, so I quote symmetrically around 15. The position cap doesn't hurt me, because every fill is at a favourable price against a known value of 15.

{"bid": 13.0, "ask": 17.0}

Card 1 is 15, the highest card in the deck, so the contract must settle at exactly 15. The informed trader's valuation is therefore 15, and the noise traders' valuations are centred on 15 with standard deviation 2.7.

I never lose money by buying below 15 or selling above 15. The only question is how far from 15 to place each quote. A quote at distance d from 15 earns d × P(a noise trader's valuation crosses it), and this is largest at d ≈ 0.75 × 2.7 ≈ 2.0. That puts the bid at about 13.0 and the ask at about 17.0, the same as my last two quotes.

